WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.869
Three efforts touch Intune and
the program keeps them apart:

2
00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:08.680
Intune-first onboarding for new devices,
this sunset for the existing fleet,

3
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.263
and regenerate-not-copy, the philosophy
for authoring the replacements.

4
00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:18.773
Microsoft's own guidance is that assessing
every GPO and every setting is likely

5
00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:24.037
impractical; identify the mandatory
settings and treat the gaps as blockers.

6
00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:28.476
The program turns that into seven
steps, each with an exit condition.

7
00:00:28.476 --> 00:00:32.950
Step 1: nothing is translated or
retired until it's inventoried.

8
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:38.775
Get-GPO-Report produces each GPO's
links, security filtering, WMI filtering

9
00:00:38.775 --> 00:00:44.225
and settings as XML, which is the
shape Group Policy analytics imports.

10
00:00:44.225 --> 00:00:48.787
Record the targeting too, because it's
the part that doesn't survive the move.

11
00:00:48.787 --> 00:00:53.428
The program's triage script sorts
each live GPO onto a target plane,

12
00:00:53.428 --> 00:00:58.997
Intune for workstations and Arc for servers,
using two classifiers side by side,

13
00:00:58.997 --> 00:01:03.300
so disagreements surface instead
of hiding behind one guess.

14
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.150
Group Policy analytics shows, per
setting, the CSP and OMA-URI behind it,

15
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:13.250
and sorts settings into ready for
migration, not supported or deprecated.

16
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:18.322
Plan around its limits: each export
under four megabytes with proper Unicode,

17
00:01:18.322 --> 00:01:23.315
the five CSPs plus preferences it
parses, non-ADMX settings read accurately

18
00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:28.150
only in English, and scope tags that
hide imports from admins without them.

19
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.238
The Migrate step is, in
Microsoft's words, best effort.

20
00:01:32.238 --> 00:01:35.287
Firewall rules and AppLocker
can't move that way.

21
00:01:35.287 --> 00:01:40.248
And when several GPOs set one setting
differently, migration stops and asks

22
00:01:40.248 --> 00:01:45.287
you to pick, which usefully surfaces the
conflicts OU precedence used to hide.

23
00:01:45.287 --> 00:01:48.742
The program treats the readiness
report as evidence that

24
00:01:48.742 --> 00:01:51.837
an equivalent exists, never
that a setting is needed.

25
00:01:51.837 --> 00:01:55.440
And it quotes no percentage:
Microsoft publishes none.
